  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Yellow Club (Clavulinopsis helvola) is a small, bright golden-yellow fungus belonging to the antler and spindle fungi family (Clavariaceae). This club-shaped or slightly branched fungus fruiting body typically grows in clusters and is one of the most recognizable yellow fungi in New England forests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Bright Yellow Color",
      "description": "Distinctively vibrant golden-yellow to pale yellow coloration throughout the fruiting body, which fades slightly with age",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Club or Spindle Shape",
      "description": "Simple, unbranched or minimally branched upright structure, typically 1-3 inches tall, with a rounded or somewhat flattened top",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Solid Interior",
      "description": "Firm, solid flesh throughout with no hollow chambers, and a smooth or finely wrinkled surface texture",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Yellow Clubs thrive in mossy, moist areas of deciduous and mixed forests, typically found on the forest floor among leaf litter and near decaying wood from late summer through fall in New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite their delicate appearance, Yellow Clubs are edible and considered a delicacy in some cuisines, though they are often too small to be worth harvesting in significant quantities.",
